WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:04.459
So what is the power of procedural rigging?

2
00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:06.500
Well, that comes from understanding

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.980
what procedural rigging is.

4
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.619
Now, the concept is to create a more efficient

5
00:00:12.619 --> 00:00:16.780
and flexible rig that will speed up the rigging workflow

6
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.500
and have the ability to be shared

7
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.859
across multiple skeletal meshes within your project.

8
00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:26.100
So it automates and accelerates the creation of controls

9
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.100
and other rig elements using the construction event,

10
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.199
which we'll look at in a bit more detail in a moment,

11
00:00:32.039 --> 00:00:35.200
but this event is essentially the event that allows you

12
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.100
to create and position things

13
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.549
like your controls automatically.

14
00:00:40.549 --> 00:00:44.450
And it simplifies the process of updating rigs

15
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.689
when a skeletal mesh changes.

16
00:00:46.689 --> 00:00:50.030
So if you've made any updates to your skeletal mesh,

17
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.270
say your character now has an extra arm

18
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:56.250
and anything else that you've changed,

19
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.570
a procedural rig can automatically re-rig itself

20
00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:03.289
as long as the bone names follow the same naming structure.

21
00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:05.450
And it doesn't matter what that naming structure is,

22
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:06.609
that's something you can define,

23
00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:10.459
but as long as you are consistent with it.

24
00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:12.719
And it's a powerful template rig to use

25
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:14.400
with all of your skeletal meshes.

26
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.599
So you can build one rig and use that

27
00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:19.959
with all of the characters in your project.

28
00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:21.599
Again, this is heavily dependent

29
00:01:21.599 --> 00:01:23.959
on your naming conventions,

30
00:01:23.959 --> 00:01:27.540
so keeping them consistent will allow for this.

31
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.840
And it's ideal if you're needing to create hundreds

32
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.200
of controls for a character,

33
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.319
so a character that might have very complex rig,

34
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:36.000
many, maybe many facial controls

35
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.079
or many like small tweaking controls.

36
00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:40.840
So procedural rigging will allow you

37
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.159
to automatically create those controls

38
00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:45.719
as well as position them.

39
00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:49.349
So it's eliminating a lot of that manual work.

40
00:01:49.349 --> 00:01:50.989
But one thing to note is

41
00:01:50.989 --> 00:01:53.590
that it's not like an easy rig button.

42
00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.870
So you do need to be mindful about things

43
00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.590
like your hierarchy and naming conventions

44
00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.030
so that you're able to share this

45
00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.430
across all of the skeletons in your project.
